     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Thu, May 17, 2018 at 9:41 AM David Perlaza &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">super...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br>Hello Dr. Chalmers,<br><br><br>Using random slope parameters, I generated some data using <a href=\"https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/mirt/versions/1.27.1/topics/simdata\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/mirt/versions/1.27.1/topics/simdata&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473677654000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0L1hyOTup0yPqxfYmW1r9A\">simdata</a>. I can recover estimates of these parameters using the <a href=\"https://rdrr.io/cran/mirt/man/coef-method.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://rdrr.io/cran/mirt/man/coef-method.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473677654000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2itGhNkZvYgG-pLCNIgcvF\">coef-method</a> on a model obtained from running the <a href=\"https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/mirt/versions/1.27.1/topics/mirt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.rdocumentation.org/packages/mirt/versions/1.27.1/topics/mirt&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792473677654000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Xjjk9qVWM8yCruxzj4ZV1\">mirt</a> function on the data.<br><br>I&#39;d just like to know if the order of estimated slope parameters matches that of the &quot;true&quot; parameters used to simulated the data.<br><br>For example, given a slope matrix <font face=\"courier new,monospace\">a<font face=\"arial,sans-serif\"> with two factors:<br></font></font><br><pre style=\"font-family:&#39;Lucida Console&#39;;font-size:10pt!important;outline:none;border:none;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap!important;line-height:15px;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;text-align:-webkit-left;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255)\">            [,1]      [,2]\r\n [1,] 1.08624628 1.3448732\r\n [2,]     ...       ...<br><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br>Do a1,a2 in the output of coef-method correspond to column 1,2 of the</span><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"> <font face=\"courier new,monospace\">a<font face=\"arial,sans-serif\"> </font></font>matrix respectively?</span><br><br>$Item_1\r\n       a1    a2      d g u\r\npar 1.318 1.369 -1.205 0 1<span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br><br>Or is the order not preserved, only the magnitudes?<br></span></pre></div></blockquote><div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div>Correct. Only asymptotically will the order of these parameters converge to the ordered pattern in the population. It&#39;s possible to estimate these parameters under constraints that the ordering is preserved, but that&#39;s actually relatively complicated to do and generally not recommended. HTH.</div><div><br></div><div>Phil</div><div> </div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
